WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.340
RUBENSTEIN: So Jason Furman is the 28th chairman
of the Council of Economic Advisors.

00:00:05.340 --> 00:00:11.130
He assumed that position in August of 2013,
after being confirmed by the United States

00:00:11.130 --> 00:00:12.520
Senate for the job.

00:00:12.520 --> 00:00:17.970
He came to that position after having served
as deputy to the NEC from more or less the

00:00:17.970 --> 00:00:23.101
beginning of the Obama administration, and
also as assistant the president for economic

00:00:23.101 --> 00:00:24.640
policy.

00:00:24.640 --> 00:00:30.550
He came to that position in the Obama administration
following having directed the economic policy

00:00:30.550 --> 00:00:36.730
part of the transition, and also serving as
economic advisor during the campaign for president

00:00:36.730 --> 00:00:38.829
that Mr. Obama-or President Obama ran.

00:00:38.829 --> 00:00:44.900
Prior to the campaign, he was running the
Hamilton Project at Brookings Institution.

00:00:44.900 --> 00:00:47.850
He also had experience in government before
that.

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:50.110
He also had worked in the Clinton administration.

00:00:50.110 --> 00:00:55.719
In the Clinton administration he was at the
NEC, at the CEA, and also he worked with Joe

00:00:55.719 --> 00:00:58.370
Stiglitz at the World Bank.

00:00:58.370 --> 00:01:03.260
He is a graduate of Dalton School in New York,
Harvard College, London School of Economics,

00:01:03.260 --> 00:01:05.560
Ph.D. from Harvard in Economics.

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:08.970
And Greg Mankiw was his thesis advisor.

00:01:08.970 --> 00:01:15.320
He is the author of hundreds of articles-not
hundreds-but many, many articles in economic

00:01:15.320 --> 00:01:16.970
policy, and written two books.

00:01:16.970 --> 00:01:19.450
And he is a native of New York.

00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:22.920
And it's my pleasure to introduce Jason Furman.

00:01:22.920 --> 00:01:26.950
Thank you.

00:01:26.950 --> 00:01:28.970
(Applause.)

00:01:28.970 --> 00:01:32.670
FURMAN: Thank you, David, for that introduction.

00:01:32.670 --> 00:01:36.720
Thank you to CFR for organizing this.

00:01:36.720 --> 00:01:42.049
I was going to spend about 10 minutes talking
about our new report about the decline in

00:01:42.049 --> 00:01:47.400
prime-age male labor force participation,
which is an issue that I address in the current

00:01:47.400 --> 00:01:51.220
issue of Foreign Affairs.

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:58.219
Part of what brought me to this is every month
I brief the president on the jobs numbers.

00:01:58.219 --> 00:02:02.790
And every month-not every month-almost every
month-I was actually in Germany last week-I

00:02:02.790 --> 00:02:08.950
get to tell him how great the number of jobs
created were, get to tell him how great the

00:02:08.950 --> 00:02:13.739
decline in the unemployment rate was, and
then there's always the labor force participation

00:02:13.739 --> 00:02:17.700
rate, which has gone in the other direction.

00:02:17.700 --> 00:02:22.489
So we've spent a lot of time over the last
many years really looking hard at that labor

00:02:22.489 --> 00:02:24.190
force participation rate.

00:02:24.190 --> 00:02:30.010
And one of the things we've learned is this
isn't just a consequence of the Great Recession

00:02:30.010 --> 00:02:34.400
or something that's happened in the recovery
from it, but a lot of the trends going on

00:02:34.400 --> 00:02:36.830
there have happened for a really long time.

00:02:36.830 --> 00:02:43.209
And you see that most clearly for men, because
there you have sort of a fixed story without

00:02:43.209 --> 00:02:45.530
some of the cultural shifts.

00:02:45.530 --> 00:02:50.910
And then you see that being paralleled more
recently, in the last 15 years or so, for

00:02:50.910 --> 00:02:51.910
women as well.

00:02:51.910 --> 00:02:52.910
So you have a handout here.

00:02:52.910 --> 00:02:58.620
And I'll take you through some of the findings
in our report, in terms of what the facts

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:04.180
are and how we interpret those facts.

00:03:04.180 --> 00:03:09.050
On that first page you have there, you see
that the male labor force participation rate

00:03:09.050 --> 00:03:13.640
has been declining steadily for 60 years.

00:03:13.640 --> 00:03:18.940
It was 98 percent in the 1950s.

00:03:18.940 --> 00:03:20.540
And now it's down to 88 percent.

00:03:20.540 --> 00:03:25.920
So if you think about that the share of prime-age
men, that's age 25 to 54, who aren't working

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:32.840
has gone from 2 percent, which is virtually
none, up to 12 percent.

00:03:32.840 --> 00:03:39.990
This isn't just people who, you know, don't
want-this isn't people who want a job and

00:03:39.990 --> 00:03:43.140
are just sitting around doing something else
in the meantime.

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:47.230
You see the share of the people who say they
do not want a job and did not work last year

00:03:47.230 --> 00:03:48.230
either.

00:03:48.230 --> 00:03:52.750
So they don't have a-they're not in the workforce
now, and for the whole of the previous year

00:03:52.750 --> 00:03:57.930
they didn't work either has actually gone
up.

00:03:57.930 --> 00:04:03.159
The most important feature of this is the
one you also have on that handout, which is

00:04:03.159 --> 00:04:07.970
it is primarily a result of people with less
education.

00:04:07.970 --> 00:04:12.989
In the 1960s, and every time we have a chart
I show you as much data as we possibly can.

00:04:12.989 --> 00:04:15.400
We go far as back as the statistics go.

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:18.350
For education that's 1964.

00:04:18.350 --> 00:04:21.650
It didn't really matter whether you had a
bachelor's degree or a high school degree,

00:04:21.650 --> 00:04:25.990
you participated at roughly 98 percent.

00:04:25.990 --> 00:04:30.050
Since then, participation has drifted down
for people with a bachelor's or higher.

00:04:30.050 --> 00:04:35.750
It's really plummeted for people with a high
school degree or less.

00:04:35.750 --> 00:04:41.930
As you see on the next set of slides, this
is an issue that a lot of OECD countries-most

00:04:41.930 --> 00:04:43.860
OECD countries have faced.

00:04:43.860 --> 00:04:47.520
But in the United States, it's been more severe.

00:04:47.520 --> 00:04:52.180
As you see on that top chart, we have the
third-lowest labor force participation rate

00:04:52.180 --> 00:04:53.940
among the OECD countries.

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:59.790
We're tied with Italy, which is probably not
the labor markets that most would aspire to,

00:04:59.790 --> 00:05:05.090
ahead of Israel, which has very significant
cultural differences, in particular certain

00:05:05.090 --> 00:05:09.840
activities are defined as not working that
a lot of men are engaged in there.

00:05:09.840 --> 00:05:15.340
We've also had a larger decline in our labor
force participation rate in all but one of

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:18.600
the OECD economies since 1990.

00:05:18.600 --> 00:05:24.250
Now, the story's a little more nuanced than
that, in that we have a lower structural unemployment

00:05:24.250 --> 00:05:26.990
rate than other countries, as you see in the
second figure.

00:05:26.990 --> 00:05:32.080
So when you combine the two of those to look
at the share that aren't employed, we're also

00:05:32.080 --> 00:05:34.200
towards the bottom of the OECD.

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:39.020
Some of the countries below us are probably
structurally ahead of the United States, they're

00:05:39.020 --> 00:05:44.259
just in deeper recessions or slower recoveries
right now.

00:05:44.259 --> 00:05:47.340
But the picture is also worrying.

00:05:47.340 --> 00:05:52.900
So if you go to the next page, you'll see
the economics lecture portion of this talk

00:05:52.900 --> 00:05:56.190
is three types of explanations.

00:05:56.190 --> 00:05:59.710
The first type of explanation is an inward
shift of the supply curve.

00:05:59.710 --> 00:06:02.560
And I'll explain what that could mean in a
moment.

00:06:02.560 --> 00:06:06.760
But the important thing here is that predicts
that the quantity of labor would go down,

00:06:06.760 --> 00:06:12.569
which is correct, but it also predicts that
the price would go up-the wages, in this case,

00:06:12.569 --> 00:06:14.190
of less-skilled workers.

00:06:14.190 --> 00:06:17.190
That's obviously not what we've seen.

00:06:17.190 --> 00:06:21.240
Demand-driven says there's less demand for
less-skilled workers.

00:06:21.240 --> 00:06:25.130
That would predict fewer of them would work,
and that their relative wages would fall.

00:06:25.130 --> 00:06:27.310
That broadly matches the data.

00:06:27.310 --> 00:06:31.410
And then finally, I'll come to some institutional
explanations, and try to flesh them out for

00:06:31.410 --> 00:06:32.410
you.

00:06:32.410 --> 00:06:35.039
But let's start on the next page with supply.

00:06:35.039 --> 00:06:40.710
One of the most obvious candidates for supply
explanation is a lot more women are working

00:06:40.710 --> 00:06:43.270
today than used to be working.

00:06:43.270 --> 00:06:47.229
And so maybe you're married to a working woman
and you can take a little bit more time to

00:06:47.229 --> 00:06:52.069
find a job, to stay in school, to care for
your children.

00:06:52.069 --> 00:06:53.690
In theory, that's possible.

00:06:53.690 --> 00:06:58.620
But if you look at that red line, despite
all these more women working today than in

00:06:58.620 --> 00:07:03.039
the 1960s, the men who aren't participating
in the workforce don't seem to be able to

00:07:03.039 --> 00:07:05.509
find them and marry them.

00:07:05.509 --> 00:07:09.259
Less than a quarter of those men and married
to working women.

00:07:09.259 --> 00:07:11.669
And that fraction has actually gone down over
time.

00:07:11.669 --> 00:07:17.360
So this explanation goes in the opposite way
of what we're trying to explain.

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:20.230
The next possibility is government programs.

00:07:20.230 --> 00:07:25.770
The most notable government program that goes
to this group is disability insurance.

00:07:25.770 --> 00:07:30.970
And without doing any economics at all, the
problem with placing too much weight on disability

00:07:30.970 --> 00:07:35.890
insurance as an explanation is the percentage
of men on disability insurance has gone up

00:07:35.890 --> 00:07:37.610
from 1 percent to 3 percent.

00:07:37.610 --> 00:07:40.220
So that's a 2 percentage point increase.

00:07:40.220 --> 00:07:44.139
The decline in the labor force participation
rate has been 7 1/2 percentage points.

00:07:44.139 --> 00:07:49.850
So at the very least you're explaining a little
bit less than-at most, I mean, you're explaining

00:07:49.850 --> 00:07:52.539
less than a third of the decline.

00:07:52.539 --> 00:07:57.190
We went through some modeling of counterfactual
scenarios, because not everyone on disability

00:07:57.190 --> 00:08:04.550
insurance would have worked, not everybody
who's on disability insurance-you know, it

00:08:04.550 --> 00:08:06.599
isn't causal in a number of different respects.

00:08:06.599 --> 00:08:07.599
And you see those three lines.

00:08:07.599 --> 00:08:12.289
And the lesson of those three lines is the
two counterfactuals look very similar to the

00:08:12.289 --> 00:08:13.289
actual.

00:08:13.289 --> 00:08:19.580
And when you try to do a causal analysis the
rise in DI explains maybe 0.3 to 0.5 percentage

00:08:19.580 --> 00:08:22.729
points of this much larger decline.

00:08:22.729 --> 00:08:28.690
And then other government programs, as you
see in the red line there, you're less likely

00:08:28.690 --> 00:08:29.690
to get.

00:08:29.690 --> 00:08:30.690
It's harder to get TANF.

00:08:30.690 --> 00:08:31.690
It's harder to get SNAP.

00:08:31.690 --> 00:08:34.599
It's harder to get just about any other government
support today.

00:08:34.599 --> 00:08:39.659
So that also pushes in the opposite direction.

00:08:39.659 --> 00:08:44.700
Having ruled out, for the most part, the supply-based
explanations, the next page gives you some

00:08:44.700 --> 00:08:46.750
sense on the demand side.

00:08:46.750 --> 00:08:51.140
And there's been a large literature on inequality
that's found that there's been skill-biased

00:08:51.140 --> 00:08:52.839
technological change.

00:08:52.839 --> 00:08:58.820
Technology complements the high-skilled people,
leading you to want to hire them and pay them

00:08:58.820 --> 00:09:02.540
more, and hasn't done the same for people
with less skills.

00:09:02.540 --> 00:09:06.800
But the process of globalization has created
a similar set of pressures.

00:09:06.800 --> 00:09:12.980
And that could manifest itself both in lower
relative wages for people with less education

00:09:12.980 --> 00:09:18.300
and in less employment prospects for them.

00:09:18.300 --> 00:09:22.760
Also we've seen, as a result of shifts in
demand, big structural changes in the economy.

00:09:22.760 --> 00:09:30.640
So manufacturing as a share of total employment
has fallen since World War II.

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:35.570
And that fall, a number of those men appear
to have, and you see this in some of the more

00:09:35.570 --> 00:09:41.750
recent studies around trade and the China
shock, haven't found jobs in other sectors

00:09:41.750 --> 00:09:43.160
of the economy.

00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:49.130
Demand, though, I don't' think can fully explain
what we've seen, because you've seen a similar

00:09:49.130 --> 00:09:54.450
demand shock when it comes to technology and
globalization in a wide range of countries-in

00:09:54.450 --> 00:09:57.410
Europe, Japan, the United States.

00:09:57.410 --> 00:10:03.060
But it's in the United States where you've
seen a larger decline in the labor force participation

00:10:03.060 --> 00:10:06.380
rate to a lower rate than in other countries.

00:10:06.380 --> 00:10:11.410
So I think it's not just the demand shock,
but now that demand shock is mediated by your

00:10:11.410 --> 00:10:13.810
economic institutions that matters.

00:10:13.810 --> 00:10:19.240
And if you turn to the next page, page 6,
those institutional explanations challenge

00:10:19.240 --> 00:10:25.440
a certain amount of the longstanding, received,
you know, orthodox wisdom around the effective

00:10:25.440 --> 00:10:27.200
functioning of labor markets.

00:10:27.200 --> 00:10:32.690
That first set of indicators shows you the
U.S. percentile in the OECD, going for growth

00:10:32.690 --> 00:10:35.149
indicators in a range of indicators.

00:10:35.149 --> 00:10:38.820
And you see the United States has the least
overall labor market regulation, the least

00:10:38.820 --> 00:10:45.080
employment protection, relatively low minimum
wage, relatively low state intervention.

00:10:45.080 --> 00:10:49.360
So all the things you're supposed to do to
make your labor market function, according

00:10:49.360 --> 00:10:54.050
to the traditional view, we do here, and we
do it better than other countries.

00:10:54.050 --> 00:10:56.940
Where we do relatively badly is nationwide
paid leave.

00:10:56.940 --> 00:10:59.529
We're the only OECD economy without it.

00:10:59.529 --> 00:11:03.829
Expenditure on active labor market policies,
job search and the like, we spend 0.1 percent

00:11:03.829 --> 00:11:07.770
of GDP compared to an OECD average of 0.6.

00:11:07.770 --> 00:11:12.330
Tax on secondary earners, subsidies for child
care, all of these types of things that I

00:11:12.330 --> 00:11:17.330
call the connective tissue that helps make
labor markets function seem like an important

00:11:17.330 --> 00:11:22.400
part, that it's not just the flexible markets
themselves but the ability to have institutions

00:11:22.400 --> 00:11:26.980
or policies that help people into jobs.

00:11:26.980 --> 00:11:34.360
The next page shows another peculiarly American
aspect of this problem, and it is the rise

00:11:34.360 --> 00:11:39.170
in the incarceration rate, which in the United
States is substantially higher than any of

00:11:39.170 --> 00:11:40.170
the OECD countries.

00:11:40.170 --> 00:11:44.610
In fact, you would have to extend this chart
out to show the Seychelles to find a country

00:11:44.610 --> 00:11:48.520
that has a higher incarceration rate than
the United States.

00:11:48.520 --> 00:11:52.610
When you're incarcerated, you are removed
from the numerator and the denominator of

00:11:52.610 --> 00:11:54.280
the labor force participation numbers.

00:11:54.280 --> 00:11:56.310
They don't count people in prison.

00:11:56.310 --> 00:11:59.899
So that actually helps make our number look
a little bit better.

00:11:59.899 --> 00:12:03.960
But when you're out of prison-and probably
about 5 (percent), 6 percent of the prime-age

00:12:03.960 --> 00:12:09.490
male population was formerly incarcerated-you
are less likely to be able to find a job,

00:12:09.490 --> 00:12:15.040
both because of the skills you lose in prison,
the stigma, but also lots of rules and regulations

00:12:15.040 --> 00:12:19.890
that govern hiring in states across the country.

00:12:19.890 --> 00:12:25.709
The final thing, though, I wanted to say is
I don't view any of this as inevitable.

00:12:25.709 --> 00:12:31.970
I think this is, as the institutional explanations
stress, the result of a range of policy choices

00:12:31.970 --> 00:12:34.889
that we have made, and we can make different
choices.

00:12:34.889 --> 00:12:38.889
Now, our goal, of course, is not a hundred
percent of people participating a hundred

00:12:38.889 --> 00:12:40.040
percent of the time.

00:12:40.040 --> 00:12:43.700
If a 19-year-old goes to college instead of
in the workforce, that's not a bad thing.

00:12:43.700 --> 00:12:48.160
And if a 75-year-old retires whereas instead
of in the workforce, that's not a bad thing

00:12:48.160 --> 00:12:49.160
either.

00:12:49.160 --> 00:12:53.891
But at 88 percent of prime-age men, I think
we're very clearly below the point where people

00:12:53.891 --> 00:12:57.600
are, you know, participating in the way that
they should.

00:12:57.600 --> 00:13:02.410
And that has ramifications not just for economic
growth but very severe ones for well-being

00:13:02.410 --> 00:13:07.570
that go beyond probably just what you would
see in standard income statistics.

00:13:07.570 --> 00:13:11.320
There's six different things that are in the
administration's agenda that would help deal

00:13:11.320 --> 00:13:12.320
with this.

00:13:12.320 --> 00:13:14.330
Aggregate demand is an important part of it.

00:13:14.330 --> 00:13:20.250
Some of the connective tissue in labor markets-community
colleges, job training, work sharing, and

00:13:20.250 --> 00:13:21.459
wage insurance.

00:13:21.459 --> 00:13:26.640
The individual tax system could help, expanding
the EITC to childless workers or workers without

00:13:26.640 --> 00:13:31.920
qualifying children, and also reducing what
is effectively a tax penalty on secondary

00:13:31.920 --> 00:13:32.920
earners.

00:13:32.920 --> 00:13:39.269
Creating more flexibility for workers in terms
of childcare, family leave, paid sick days

00:13:39.269 --> 00:13:44.480
are all things that are more often associated
with female labor force participation but

00:13:44.480 --> 00:13:46.630
would help with male as well.

00:13:46.630 --> 00:13:51.300
There's a range of things which are much bigger
policies than just this issue alone, like

00:13:51.300 --> 00:13:55.500
education, immigration reform, and criminal
justice reform, that would also have very

00:13:55.500 --> 00:13:57.700
positive impacts for this.

00:13:57.700 --> 00:14:05.110
And then finally, the conclusion I would make
is this idea that you have to accept large

00:14:05.110 --> 00:14:11.050
increases in inequality because you don't
want to interfere with labor markets and the

00:14:11.050 --> 00:14:16.910
way they function is something that I think
is basically debunked by this analysis.

00:14:16.910 --> 00:14:22.160
So you basically lose the excuse to not do
anything about inequality because you're afraid

00:14:22.160 --> 00:14:23.870
it would mess up your labor market.

00:14:23.870 --> 00:14:27.850
And in fact, steps like raising the minimum
wage or collective bargaining and voice in

00:14:27.850 --> 00:14:33.350
labor markets could actually raise wages and
expand labor supply and raise labor force

00:14:33.350 --> 00:14:35.120
participation.

00:14:35.120 --> 00:14:40.550
This is of course only one of the many economic
issues that we're trying to address, but it

00:14:40.550 --> 00:14:46.630
does affect some of the big ones in terms
of growth, inequality, and just the perception

00:14:46.630 --> 00:14:48.600
overall about how the economy is doing.

00:14:48.600 --> 00:14:57.560
And I'm happy to talk about any of that with
you and everyone else.

00:14:57.560 --> 00:15:00.880
(Applause.)

00:15:00.880 --> 00:15:07.589
RUBENSTEIN: I have observed over the years
when presidents of the United States have

00:15:07.589 --> 00:15:12.899
press conferences they give a little talk
in the beginning, and then the first questions

00:15:12.899 --> 00:15:15.010
bear no relation to what the president talked
about.

00:15:15.010 --> 00:15:16.010
(Laughter.)

00:15:16.010 --> 00:15:17.010
So I'm not going to do that.

00:15:17.010 --> 00:15:19.329
I'll get to some other questions, but I did
want to ask a few things about what you talked

00:15:19.329 --> 00:15:21.420
about, but we have so many economic issues
to cover.

00:15:21.420 --> 00:15:27.130
So on this, can you explain something that
was a little confusing to me until I did a

00:15:27.130 --> 00:15:32.029
little research on it, but when the Bureau
of Labor Statistics comes out every month

00:15:32.029 --> 00:15:36.029
and says, here's our unemployment rate and
here is the labor participation rate, they

00:15:36.029 --> 00:15:41.860
will say, well, we have a 66 percent historical
labor participation rate; now it's 62.3 percent

00:15:41.860 --> 00:15:42.860
or something.

00:15:42.860 --> 00:15:47.459
You're using numbers for the prime-age category,
so you have-you're dealing with the different

00:15:47.459 --> 00:15:48.459
numbers.

00:15:48.459 --> 00:15:51.300
Can you explain the difference between the
numbers that we often get associated with

00:15:51.300 --> 00:15:56.060
labor rate participation, the 66 (percent)
number-62.3 (percent) versus the 88 (percent)

00:15:56.060 --> 00:15:57.130
number you're dealing with, OK?

00:15:57.130 --> 00:15:58.130
FURMAN: Yeah.

00:15:58.130 --> 00:16:02.440
Yeah, I mean, you can divide it by age and
you can divide it by gender.

00:16:02.440 --> 00:16:04.949
And there have been very different dynamics
for all of those.

00:16:04.949 --> 00:16:09.769
So if you look at women, their labor force
participation rate rose from when the data

00:16:09.769 --> 00:16:15.380
started in 1948 through about 2000, then peaked
and started falling.

00:16:15.380 --> 00:16:19.660
If you look at younger people, people below
the age of 25, you've seen their labor force

00:16:19.660 --> 00:16:24.400
participation rates falling but you also see
more of them in college, so the share that

00:16:24.400 --> 00:16:26.980
are either working or in school has stayed
the same.

00:16:26.980 --> 00:16:31.730
You look at older people, people over 55,
and there has been a set of shifts in terms

00:16:31.730 --> 00:16:35.590
of the types of jobs people have, the type
of health they have, and rules around Social

00:16:35.590 --> 00:16:36.590
Security.

00:16:36.590 --> 00:16:38.740
So their labor force participation has been
rising.

00:16:38.740 --> 00:16:43.480
So there's so many different stories going
on based on different institutions and cultures.

00:16:43.480 --> 00:16:48.269
That's why here we chose to focus on men between
the age of 25 and 54.

00:16:48.269 --> 00:16:53.130
RUBENSTEIN: OK, so between 25 and 54 is what
your category is, but when the Bureau of Labor

00:16:53.130 --> 00:16:56.839
Statistics comes out they use the-they use
everybody in the workforce, is that right?

00:16:56.839 --> 00:17:00.110
And is that the better-
FURMAN: That's their headline, although if

00:17:00.110 --> 00:17:02.389
you page through their thing, they do it.

00:17:02.389 --> 00:17:06.410
And the word "prime-age men" was not the word
I came up with.

00:17:06.410 --> 00:17:07.410
RUBENSTEIN: OK.

00:17:07.410 --> 00:17:08.730
FURMAN: So for anyone who is not included
in that age bracket-

00:17:08.730 --> 00:17:10.559
RUBENSTEIN: All right, so-(laughter)-
FURMAN: -you should address your complaints

00:17:10.559 --> 00:17:11.579
to them, not to me.

00:17:11.579 --> 00:17:12.579
RUBENSTEIN: All right.

00:17:12.579 --> 00:17:15.949
OK, so-no, I've already passed that concern
over the years when I got past that age.

00:17:15.949 --> 00:17:16.949
(Laughter.)

00:17:16.949 --> 00:17:22.720
So let me ask you about the issue of why this
is such a bad thing, because the theory would

00:17:22.720 --> 00:17:28.500
be that if you don't have to work and you're
doing well-and maybe our economy is so wealthy

00:17:28.500 --> 00:17:30.110
that people just don't feel a need to work.

00:17:30.110 --> 00:17:32.580
They have inherited money, they've made enough
money.

00:17:32.580 --> 00:17:36.460
So why is it clear that having a lower participation
rate is a bad thing?

00:17:36.460 --> 00:17:41.059
FURMAN: I think it depends on who it is and
what their circumstances are.

00:17:41.059 --> 00:17:48.990
So if we looked at a chart and we saw college-educated
men who are married to working women are the

00:17:48.990 --> 00:17:55.900
ones who are not participating, you know,
I'd be a bit jealous-(laughter)-but say that

00:17:55.900 --> 00:17:59.400
could be a perfectly great thing, and I'm
certainly not going to necessarily worry about

00:17:59.400 --> 00:18:04.240
it as an economic policy and am not going
to judge the choices people make.

00:18:04.240 --> 00:18:08.201
When you see it's people with a high school
degree or less, you see it's people who are

00:18:08.201 --> 00:18:13.410
disproportionately in poverty, you look at
the time use-you don't see them spending more

00:18:13.410 --> 00:18:18.510
time, you know, helping with children, helping
around the house-and then you look at the

00:18:18.510 --> 00:18:25.470
studies of what, you know, prolonged unemployment
or non-employment does to people psychologically

00:18:25.470 --> 00:18:30.040
and in terms of their ability to find future
employment, I think at that point it becomes

00:18:30.040 --> 00:18:31.040
a worry.

00:18:31.040 --> 00:18:35.600
RUBENSTEIN: So in your article in the Foreign
Affairs journal that the Council publishes-you

00:18:35.600 --> 00:18:40.140
have an article about this, and in that article
you do talk about the licensing restrictions

00:18:40.140 --> 00:18:42.310
that we often have to get jobs in the United
States.

00:18:42.310 --> 00:18:44.549
You might elaborate on why that's a bit of
a problem.

00:18:44.549 --> 00:18:45.809
FURMAN: Yeah.

00:18:45.809 --> 00:18:49.760
We used to have about 5 percent of jobs in
this country you need a license for.

00:18:49.760 --> 00:18:55.110
And I don't think any of us have a problem
with doctors, for example, needing a license.

00:18:55.110 --> 00:18:56.720
Now it's up to 25 percent.

00:18:56.720 --> 00:19:02.150
And while I respect people who would choose
to go to a florist or interior decorator that's

00:19:02.150 --> 00:19:08.230
licensed, you know, I might be willing to
take my chances when it comes to those professions.

00:19:08.230 --> 00:19:10.740
This makes it harder for people to get jobs.

00:19:10.740 --> 00:19:13.990
It makes it harder for them to move from state
to state.

00:19:13.990 --> 00:19:21.260
And it especially hurts a few groups, one
of them formerly incarcerated but also military

00:19:21.260 --> 00:19:27.000
families and immigrants, and it makes the
overall labor market less fluid.

00:19:27.000 --> 00:19:32.080
And when there's less people moving around,
moving between jobs, leaving jobs, finding

00:19:32.080 --> 00:19:36.040
jobs, it means if you're unlucky enough to
lose a job in a recession, it's going to be

00:19:36.040 --> 00:19:40.980
harder for you to cycle back into a new one,
and so recessions could potentially have more

00:19:40.980 --> 00:19:42.860
long-term impacts on the labor market too.

00:19:42.860 --> 00:19:43.860
RUBENSTEIN: OK.

00:19:43.860 --> 00:19:49.710
Now, in your article you also talk about France,
not normally cited as a great model for economic

00:19:49.710 --> 00:19:50.710
capitalism.

00:19:50.710 --> 00:19:57.010
But why are so many men and women in France
employed, relative to the United States, in

00:19:57.010 --> 00:19:58.010
your view?

00:19:58.010 --> 00:20:02.230
FURMAN: You know, I was asking two of the
top labor economists in France that question

00:20:02.230 --> 00:20:06.990
a few weeks ago and they were somewhat puzzled
to find out that they came out ahead of the

00:20:06.990 --> 00:20:07.990
United States.

00:20:07.990 --> 00:20:12.559
And I want to be completely clear: I do not
think, you know, that we should be adopting

00:20:12.559 --> 00:20:16.400
French labor market rules, and take everything
they do in France and apply it here.

00:20:16.400 --> 00:20:20.450
What I do think, though, is that when it comes
to the function of labor markets, we need

00:20:20.450 --> 00:20:25.230
to be less sure that we already know the answer
and a little bit more humble about, you know,

00:20:25.230 --> 00:20:28.140
our system, some of its shortcomings, and
need for change.

00:20:28.140 --> 00:20:31.990
RUBENSTEIN: Now, the trend you've talked about,
is it a trend that has been going on for quite

00:20:31.990 --> 00:20:36.690
some time, and it's not something that any
administration-yours in the remaining six

00:20:36.690 --> 00:20:40.520
months you have or even the next administration-can
have a dramatic effect on?

00:20:40.520 --> 00:20:44.460
Or do you think somebody can have a dramatic
effect in one, two, or three years in changing

00:20:44.460 --> 00:20:45.460
these numbers?

00:20:45.460 --> 00:20:49.250
FURMAN: You know, certainly a lot of the ideas
we're talking of here are obviously ideas

00:20:49.250 --> 00:20:52.760
for the future, and the president in his last
State of the Union said he was talking about

00:20:52.760 --> 00:20:54.720
not just this year.

00:20:54.720 --> 00:20:57.990
I think it would all add up and make a difference
over time.

00:20:57.990 --> 00:21:01.560
But I think, frankly, a lot of economic policy,
whether you're talking about productivity

00:21:01.560 --> 00:21:07.410
growth, inequality, or participation, is about
a few tenths here, a few tenths there, and

00:21:07.410 --> 00:21:10.630
then they accumulate over time, and eventually
it does make a big difference.

00:21:10.630 --> 00:21:14.950
RUBENSTEIN: All right, so if I could shift
to another subject for a moment, some people

00:21:14.950 --> 00:21:21.450
think that the vote in England this week will
be a very significant economic event for the

00:21:21.450 --> 00:21:22.450
world.

00:21:22.450 --> 00:21:28.789
What is your view on the impact of a so-called
vote to exit the EU would be, on our economy

00:21:28.789 --> 00:21:30.440
and the British and the-and the European economy?

00:21:30.440 --> 00:21:34.600
FURMAN: All right, so just about every institution
that I'm aware of, with the possible exception

00:21:34.600 --> 00:21:40.179
of the Council of Economic Advisors, has done
some estimate of the economic impact of Brexit.

00:21:40.179 --> 00:21:44.770
And the thing that they all have in common
is a minus sign in front of them.

00:21:44.770 --> 00:21:48.300
You know, you can see minus-1, minus-2, minus-8
percent on growth.

00:21:48.300 --> 00:21:50.880
You see a range of things.

00:21:50.880 --> 00:21:55.650
Part of why you don't know the answer as to
just how bad it would be for the economy is

00:21:55.650 --> 00:21:58.570
you don't know what sort of deal they would
be able to negotiate.

00:21:58.570 --> 00:22:04.110
But just classic trade theory gives you already
a few percentage points in terms of extra

00:22:04.110 --> 00:22:08.830
barriers, less ability to locate people and
businesses where they should.

00:22:08.830 --> 00:22:12.230
The thing that's scary, though, isn't the
classical trade theory, which plays out over

00:22:12.230 --> 00:22:14.120
a longer period of time.

00:22:14.120 --> 00:22:18.590
It's just look how much markets are moving
right now already on little bits of news and

00:22:18.590 --> 00:22:19.590
polls.

00:22:19.590 --> 00:22:24.630
Imagine, you know, what the potential impact
of an actual event could be on capital flows,

00:22:24.630 --> 00:22:28.679
exchange rates, and spilling out periphery,
et cetera.

00:22:28.679 --> 00:22:33.159
So I think this is a(n), you know, unnecessary
risk for the global economy.

00:22:33.159 --> 00:22:36.720
RUBENSTEIN: You think we could have had a
more favorable impact on the vote if we had

00:22:36.720 --> 00:22:41.300
said pull out, and then the British in response
to that would say-(laughter)-we don't want

00:22:41.300 --> 00:22:43.590
to listen to the Yankees, we'll do the opposite?

00:22:43.590 --> 00:22:44.590
(Laughter.)

00:22:44.590 --> 00:22:45.590
That strategy wasn't considered?

00:22:45.590 --> 00:22:48.770
FURMAN: I will-I will leave that for our international
political strategists.

00:22:48.770 --> 00:22:49.770
RUBENSTEIN: OK.

00:22:49.770 --> 00:22:54.360
So, since the Obama administration's been
in office, you've been dealing with the effects

00:22:54.360 --> 00:22:56.730
of the recession and recovering from that.

00:22:56.730 --> 00:22:59.680
The recession technically ended in June of
2009.

00:22:59.680 --> 00:23:03.710
Typically, we have recessions every seven
years in the United States, since World War

00:23:03.710 --> 00:23:08.120
II, on average; obviously, some years you
go longer than that, or some periods.

00:23:08.120 --> 00:23:14.929
But seven years from June of 2009 is June
of 2016, which happens to be this month.

00:23:14.929 --> 00:23:20.400
So do you think we escape the seven-year jinx
and we can go for quite a while longer before

00:23:20.400 --> 00:23:21.400
we get into a recession?

00:23:21.400 --> 00:23:23.880
You don't see anything on the horizon that
will give us a recession?

00:23:23.880 --> 00:23:31.429
FURMAN: I think the economic evidence is that
recession's a little bit like what an economist

00:23:31.429 --> 00:23:37.169
would call a Poisson process, of each year
there is a certain probability that a recession

00:23:37.169 --> 00:23:42.620
happens, and that probability doesn't go up
over time and doesn't go down over time.

00:23:42.620 --> 00:23:47.940
And another way of putting that is expansions
don't die of old age.

00:23:47.940 --> 00:23:52.860
So I still-so I think that, as a general economic
matter, is the way I think about it.

00:23:52.860 --> 00:23:58.420
And then specifically, when I look at our
economy now, I think we are really far towards

00:23:58.420 --> 00:23:59.420
our recovery.

00:23:59.420 --> 00:24:02.660
We're much further than most of the other
economies that were affected by the recession.

00:24:02.660 --> 00:24:04.540
But we're still not all the way there yet.

00:24:04.540 --> 00:24:06.270
I still see slack in our labor market.

00:24:06.270 --> 00:24:08.050
I still see more potential.

00:24:08.050 --> 00:24:11.690
RUBENSTEIN: Since World War II, when we've
had recoveries from the recessions, we've

00:24:11.690 --> 00:24:17.140
typically grown in the recovery period of
time 3 percent and 4 percent per annum.

00:24:17.140 --> 00:24:20.980
In the Obama administration, we've never had
a year of 3 percent GDP growth.

00:24:20.980 --> 00:24:25.390
Do you think the days of 3 percent or more
economic growth are behind us because our

00:24:25.390 --> 00:24:27.900
economy's so big and we just can't grow at
3 and 4 percent anymore?

00:24:27.900 --> 00:24:30.279
Or are there other reasons for that lower
growth?

00:24:30.279 --> 00:24:34.380
FURMAN: I think the biggest difference is
just demographics-that if you look at, again,

00:24:34.380 --> 00:24:40.010
this prime age population, 25 to 54, in the
1980s-and I'm doing this from memory and might

00:24:40.010 --> 00:24:44.080
have this wrong-I think it was growing at
about 1 1/2 percent per year.

00:24:44.080 --> 00:24:49.970
Now that age group is contracting and, you
know, the age group that's growing is the

00:24:49.970 --> 00:24:51.470
people who are going into retirement.

00:24:51.470 --> 00:24:56.660
So I think the biggest headwind for our growth
has been demographic.

00:24:56.660 --> 00:25:00.760
And that's partly why this labor force participation
issue is so important, because the response

00:25:00.760 --> 00:25:05.100
to demography can be to just accept slower
growth, which is what they appear to be doing

00:25:05.100 --> 00:25:09.130
in Japan; it can be to try to encourage more
people to have children, which they're doing

00:25:09.130 --> 00:25:14.160
in France; or it can be to try to get a larger
fraction of your population working or bring

00:25:14.160 --> 00:25:15.160
new people in.

00:25:15.160 --> 00:25:16.740
And that's why I think those steps are so
important.

00:25:16.740 --> 00:25:20.240
RUBENSTEIN: So if Janet Yellen were to say
to you, you know, I'm not sure what to do

00:25:20.240 --> 00:25:25.450
with interest rates the remainder of the year,
I need to know where the economy's going,

00:25:25.450 --> 00:25:27.770
what is your view on the economy and what
do you think I should do about interest rates,

00:25:27.770 --> 00:25:28.860
what would you tell her?

00:25:28.860 --> 00:25:33.960
FURMAN: I would tell her that I respect her
independence and-(laughter)-she has an outstanding

00:25:33.960 --> 00:25:34.960
staff.

00:25:34.960 --> 00:25:35.960
RUBENSTEIN: OK.

00:25:35.960 --> 00:25:40.070
This year you would project economic growth
for the United States to be roughly 1 1/2

00:25:40.070 --> 00:25:42.090
percent, or something like that?

00:25:42.090 --> 00:25:46.870
FURMAN: Our last forecast was completed in
November of last year, published in February

00:25:46.870 --> 00:25:48.200
in this year.

00:25:48.200 --> 00:25:51.799
And even if I could remember it, I'd claim
to have forgotten it because the number would

00:25:51.799 --> 00:25:53.350
be considerably higher than that.

00:25:53.350 --> 00:25:55.650
But I still think above 2 percent for this
year.

00:25:55.650 --> 00:25:56.650
RUBENSTEIN: Above 2 percent?

00:25:56.650 --> 00:25:57.650
FURMAN: Yeah.

00:25:57.650 --> 00:25:58.650
RUBENSTEIN: OK.

00:25:58.650 --> 00:25:59.650
And what do you think-
FURMAN: The second quarter, as you know is

00:25:59.650 --> 00:26:03.370
tracking, you know, north of 2 1/2 percent
in a range of tracking indexes.

00:26:03.370 --> 00:26:04.370
RUBENSTEIN: OK.

00:26:04.370 --> 00:26:07.809
What do you think, by the end of this administration,
the unemployment rate will be?

00:26:07.809 --> 00:26:10.370
FURMAN: A lot lower than it was when it began.

00:26:10.370 --> 00:26:11.370
(Laughter.)

00:26:11.370 --> 00:26:12.370
RUBENSTEIN: OK.

00:26:12.370 --> 00:26:13.370
All right.

00:26:13.370 --> 00:26:15.450
But do you think the unemployment rate is
as relevant as it used to be?

00:26:15.450 --> 00:26:19.350
Because it measures people who looked for
jobs in the last 30 days, as opposed to the

00:26:19.350 --> 00:26:21.080
labor force participation rate and other things.

00:26:21.080 --> 00:26:25.520
So when it goes below 5 percent, is it as
important an indicator as it once was, in

00:26:25.520 --> 00:26:26.520
your view?

00:26:26.520 --> 00:26:31.020
FURMAN: I think if there's only one labor
market indicator you're looking at, I think

00:26:31.020 --> 00:26:37.190
you're much better off looking at the unemployment
rate because it really attempts to scale by

00:26:37.190 --> 00:26:40.679
people who want jobs and can't find them.

00:26:40.679 --> 00:26:44.991
Once you look at things like employment-population
ratio or labor force participation, you need

00:26:44.991 --> 00:26:48.330
to make sure you're getting straight in your
head all these issues we've been talking about

00:26:48.330 --> 00:26:52.750
of longer-term trends and cultural shifts
and shifting age structure and a whole bunch

00:26:52.750 --> 00:26:53.750
of things.

00:26:53.750 --> 00:26:57.740
I think if you do that well, that brings you
another, richer perspective.

00:26:57.740 --> 00:26:59.740
But if you do it badly, it can mislead you.

00:26:59.740 --> 00:27:03.039
RUBENSTEIN: Now, you've worked for President
Clinton and President Obama.

00:27:03.039 --> 00:27:05.760
Who understands economic matters better?

00:27:05.760 --> 00:27:06.760
(Laughter.)

00:27:06.760 --> 00:27:08.760
FURMAN: You should ask Gene Sperling.

00:27:08.760 --> 00:27:09.760
(Laughter.)

00:27:09.760 --> 00:27:10.760
RUBENSTEIN: OK.

00:27:10.760 --> 00:27:11.760
All right.

00:27:11.760 --> 00:27:16.289
So, with respect to the administration, you've
got six months to go.

00:27:16.289 --> 00:27:22.470
Are there many economic policies that you
think you can pursue and adopt and get implemented

00:27:22.470 --> 00:27:24.279
before the administration's over?

00:27:24.279 --> 00:27:27.539
Is there any-what would you say your highest
economic priorities are now?

00:27:27.539 --> 00:27:32.370
FURMAN: One thing we can certainly pass is
the Trans-Pacific Partnership.

00:27:32.370 --> 00:27:36.690
And that's very important economically, very
important broader than that.

00:27:36.690 --> 00:27:43.460
There is a range of other issues-whether it's
Zika, opioids, Puerto Rico-that are important

00:27:43.460 --> 00:27:46.620
economically going through Congress.

00:27:46.620 --> 00:27:47.860
There's global economic management.

00:27:47.860 --> 00:27:53.220
We were just in China a few weeks ago engaging
with them on the big issues around exchange

00:27:53.220 --> 00:27:56.870
rate, capacity, intellectual property, the
BIT.

00:27:56.870 --> 00:28:01.049
So there's a range of international economic
relationships to put forward.

00:28:01.049 --> 00:28:04.140
But one thing that we're trying to do at the
Council of Economic Advisors, in addition

00:28:04.140 --> 00:28:09.590
to helping out with all of that, is things
like this report that we're discussing today,

00:28:09.590 --> 00:28:14.140
that I don't expect to be the basis for legislative
action, you know, tomorrow or the next day,

00:28:14.140 --> 00:28:17.590
but I do hope change what is possible in the
future.

00:28:17.590 --> 00:28:20.991
RUBENSTEIN: So tell us, when you're the chairman
of the Council of Economic Advisors, your

00:28:20.991 --> 00:28:23.590
job is to advise the president on economic
matters.

00:28:23.590 --> 00:28:26.789
So, does he call you up and say, how is the
economy doing today?

00:28:26.789 --> 00:28:29.700
Or how do you actually get information to
him?

00:28:29.700 --> 00:28:33.080
And does he like to hear more about it, or
does he read more about it, or how do you

00:28:33.080 --> 00:28:34.470
communicate with him?

00:28:34.470 --> 00:28:36.980
FURMAN: We send him a lot of memos.

00:28:36.980 --> 00:28:43.940
Every major piece of data-and "major" is defined
quite broadly-we get a day in advance.

00:28:43.940 --> 00:28:46.760
We write up a one-page memo with our analysis.

00:28:46.760 --> 00:28:47.830
That goes to him.

00:28:47.830 --> 00:28:50.560
And, in our experience, he really reads those.

00:28:50.560 --> 00:28:53.100
We do a lot of one-off memos on topics.

00:28:53.100 --> 00:28:58.500
So labor force participation, for example,
we a few years ago did a memo that-you know,

00:28:58.500 --> 00:29:00.860
on a range of issues there.

00:29:00.860 --> 00:29:05.539
But we also meet with him, both short meetings
to brief him on the jobs number or the GDP

00:29:05.539 --> 00:29:10.450
number, longer meetings that could either
be update on the global economy or on a particular

00:29:10.450 --> 00:29:11.450
policy issue.

00:29:11.450 --> 00:29:16.160
And in my experience, he's a great reader
and a great discusser.

00:29:16.160 --> 00:29:20.374
So, you know, the best is, you know, get him
a memo in advance and then have a meeting,

00:29:20.374 --> 00:29:26.149
and the meeting isn't plodding through a bunch
of facts really slowly but jumping straight

00:29:26.149 --> 00:29:29.490
to discussing, you know, what the implications
are and what you can do about it.

00:29:29.490 --> 00:29:31.529
RUBENSTEIN: So suppose he says something that's
wrong.

00:29:31.529 --> 00:29:35.140
Do you say, well, Mr. President, you might
not understand that completely?

00:29:35.140 --> 00:29:37.549
Or, you know, what's the best way to phrase
it to-

00:29:37.549 --> 00:29:39.130
FURMAN: You know, fortunately that's just
never happened.

00:29:39.130 --> 00:29:40.130
(Laughter.)

00:29:40.130 --> 00:29:42.210
RUBENSTEIN: Never happened, never happened,
ah.

00:29:42.210 --> 00:29:43.210
OK.

00:29:43.210 --> 00:29:48.010
So if the next president of the United States,
whoever he or she might be, were to call you

00:29:48.010 --> 00:29:52.539
up and say you've been in the White House,
actually, for eight years now; you know the

00:29:52.539 --> 00:29:57.510
economy pretty well; tell me what I should
worry about right away, at the beginning of

00:29:57.510 --> 00:30:01.610
my administration-what should I do to jumpstart
the economy or get the economy in pretty good

00:30:01.610 --> 00:30:05.640
shape-what would you recommend to either party
to do?

00:30:05.640 --> 00:30:07.760
FURMAN: You can always worry.

00:30:07.760 --> 00:30:11.440
You know, we were talking about Brexit before,
so I'm worried about, you know, that this

00:30:11.440 --> 00:30:12.490
week.

00:30:12.490 --> 00:30:15.059
You know, you can worry about the 2nd quarter
GDP numbers.

00:30:15.059 --> 00:30:17.890
You can worry about the next jobs number,
all of that.

00:30:17.890 --> 00:30:23.740
But I think it's really important to not just
let those urgent matters swamp your attention,

00:30:23.740 --> 00:30:25.350
and focus on some things that are really important.

00:30:25.350 --> 00:30:30.789
And I think the three biggest drivers of middle
class incomes, which is our goal in economic

00:30:30.789 --> 00:30:35.320
policy-middle class and people trying to get
into the middle class-are productivity growth,

00:30:35.320 --> 00:30:36.870
inequality, and participation.

00:30:36.870 --> 00:30:39.450
So this report is about one of those three.

00:30:39.450 --> 00:30:43.840
But focus on, you know, what you can do to
add two-tenths of percent per year to our

00:30:43.840 --> 00:30:48.080
productivity growth rate, and that'll take
care of a lot of other problems over time.

00:30:48.080 --> 00:30:53.799
RUBENSTEIN: And income inequality, on that
subject, do you believe that there is a growing

00:30:53.799 --> 00:30:54.799
gap in income equality?

00:30:54.799 --> 00:31:01.279
Do you think it's a result of the recession
just creating this greater income equality,

00:31:01.279 --> 00:31:05.370
and afterwards the effects were such that
the wealthy got wealthier and the poor got

00:31:05.370 --> 00:31:06.370
poorer?

00:31:06.370 --> 00:31:08.750
What was the reason that the income inequality
got to be so great?

00:31:08.750 --> 00:31:10.210
FURMAN: That's a long topic.

00:31:10.210 --> 00:31:14.240
Income inequality is a lot higher now than
it was in the 1970s.

00:31:14.240 --> 00:31:18.430
So it's a longer-term thing, not quite as
long term as the participation, but it's a

00:31:18.430 --> 00:31:20.760
longer-term thing.

00:31:20.760 --> 00:31:23.899
It's big enough that I think there's space
for a lot of explanation.

00:31:23.899 --> 00:31:25.750
Some people have, like, this is my one explanation.

00:31:25.750 --> 00:31:27.720
They get really mad at anyone who has another.

00:31:27.720 --> 00:31:32.220
I'm much more ecumenical and think, you know,
we can accommodate them all.

00:31:32.220 --> 00:31:34.220
So technology is certainly one factor.

00:31:34.220 --> 00:31:38.649
A slowdown in educational attainment is another
factor.

00:31:38.649 --> 00:31:39.649
Globalization has put pressure on it.

00:31:39.649 --> 00:31:41.029
The decline of labor unions.

00:31:41.029 --> 00:31:43.000
The decline of the minimum wage.

00:31:43.000 --> 00:31:45.510
Potentially some reduced competition in the
economy.

00:31:45.510 --> 00:31:49.010
I think all of those have combined together
to cause it.

00:31:49.010 --> 00:31:50.010
RUBENSTEIN: OK.

00:31:50.010 --> 00:31:52.860
Is deflation a problem that you worry about
very much now?

00:31:52.860 --> 00:31:57.850
FURMAN: I'm not, frankly, that worried about
it right now in the United States.

00:31:57.850 --> 00:32:03.519
We see our inflation rate moving up, not to
a point that worries me, but not in a deflationary

00:32:03.519 --> 00:32:05.169
direction at all.

00:32:05.169 --> 00:32:10.040
It's very different, of course, in Japan and
the euro zone, where I think it's a much greater

00:32:10.040 --> 00:32:11.040
concern.

00:32:11.040 --> 00:32:14.279
RUBENSTEIN: And in China, you said you were
there recently, what is your view of the likely

00:32:14.279 --> 00:32:16.410
growth rate in China this year?

00:32:16.410 --> 00:32:18.190
FURMAN: Whatever they want.

00:32:18.190 --> 00:32:19.190
(Laughter.)

00:32:19.190 --> 00:32:23.100
No-
RUBENSTEIN: Well, Alan Greenspan used to say

00:32:23.100 --> 00:32:26.710
that the advantage of dealing with Chinese
numbers is you know them before the end of

00:32:26.710 --> 00:32:28.750
the month.

00:32:28.750 --> 00:32:33.309
But your view is-
FURMAN: No, you know, they have a big set

00:32:33.309 --> 00:32:37.340
of economic challenges in terms of adjustment
they're trying to make to a consumer-oriented

00:32:37.340 --> 00:32:39.840
economy, a service oriented economy.

00:32:39.840 --> 00:32:45.220
They have a lot of resources to help meet
those challenges.

00:32:45.220 --> 00:32:51.850
But you know, they're going to need to negotiate
them and then, you know, that's all against

00:32:51.850 --> 00:32:56.020
a backdrop of it's harder to grow when you're
closer to the frontier.

00:32:56.020 --> 00:33:00.000
And it's harder to grow when your demography
is as unfavorable as the Chinese demography

00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:01.000
is.

00:33:01.000 --> 00:33:04.059
So some of the advantages they've had over
the last decades they're not going to have.

00:33:04.059 --> 00:33:09.289
So they're going to need-you know, figure
out, you know, more innovation, more consumer-driven,

00:33:09.289 --> 00:33:10.289
more services.

00:33:10.289 --> 00:33:11.289
RUBENSTEIN: What about Japan's demography?

00:33:11.289 --> 00:33:12.970
Are you more worried about theirs?

00:33:12.970 --> 00:33:18.039
FURMAN: Oh, now, theirs is, I mean, so worrying
you might give up worrying.

00:33:18.039 --> 00:33:22.789
But they've-but, you know, one thing Japan's
done well, actually, is they've done a concerted

00:33:22.789 --> 00:33:25.549
effort to increase women's participation in
the workforce.

00:33:25.549 --> 00:33:27.960
And that's been reasonably successful.

00:33:27.960 --> 00:33:31.830
And for prime-age women, they're basically
tied with the United States now, after being

00:33:31.830 --> 00:33:32.830
way behind us.

00:33:32.830 --> 00:33:35.799
RUBENSTEIN: So you're trained to be a professional
economist, you are.

00:33:35.799 --> 00:33:40.100
So if you could have dinner with any person
who's ever been an economist, and just have

00:33:40.100 --> 00:33:45.900
chance to spend dinner with that person, who
would you want to spend dinner with, other

00:33:45.900 --> 00:33:48.250
than the people you work for?

00:33:48.250 --> 00:33:49.360
FURMAN: Oh, all right, good.

00:33:49.360 --> 00:33:53.130
I was about to name one of them, but that
would be embarrassing to say.

00:33:53.130 --> 00:33:55.600
So I think John Maynard Keynes, just his breadth.

00:33:55.600 --> 00:34:00.330
I mean, you could spend a bunch of time talking
about economics, and then you could switch

00:34:00.330 --> 00:34:01.730
to a range of other topics.

00:34:01.730 --> 00:34:02.730
RUBENSTEIN: OK.

00:34:02.730 --> 00:34:03.760
FURMAN: He was an investor too-successful
too.

00:34:03.760 --> 00:34:04.760
RUBENSTEIN: He was.

00:34:04.760 --> 00:34:06.720
Well, he lost money-
FURMAN: Successful, unsuccessful, successful,

00:34:06.720 --> 00:34:07.720
unsuccessful.

00:34:07.720 --> 00:34:08.720
RUBENSTEIN: Yeah.

00:34:08.720 --> 00:34:09.720
He had his ups and downs.

00:34:09.720 --> 00:34:10.720
But OK.

00:34:10.720 --> 00:34:11.720
All right.

00:34:11.720 --> 00:34:12.720
(Laughter.)

00:34:12.720 --> 00:34:13.720
So let's have some questions.

00:34:13.720 --> 00:34:14.720
OK.

00:34:14.720 --> 00:34:15.720
Just please right here.

00:34:15.720 --> 00:34:16.839
Identify yourself, your affiliation, and have
a simple question.

00:34:16.839 --> 00:34:17.839
(Laughter.)

00:34:17.839 --> 00:34:19.040
Q: Thank you very much.

00:34:19.040 --> 00:34:22.320
It was a great discussion.

00:34:22.320 --> 00:34:24.829
That's a compliment to you, David, as well,
Jason.

00:34:24.829 --> 00:34:25.980
RUBENSTEIN: All right.

00:34:25.980 --> 00:34:26.980
Thank you.

00:34:26.980 --> 00:34:29.280
Q: And my name is Paula Stern.

00:34:29.280 --> 00:34:32.179
And I'm affiliated with The Stern Group, Incorporated.

00:34:32.179 --> 00:34:35.590
And my-
RUBENSTEIN: And a refugee of the Carter administration.

00:34:35.590 --> 00:34:38.659
Q: Carter, and then Reagan designated me chairman.

00:34:38.659 --> 00:34:40.950
So I'm bipartisan.

00:34:40.950 --> 00:34:45.670
And we all are, as CFR is very definitely.

00:34:45.670 --> 00:34:51.460
My question-this was so wonderful, as was
the last CEA report, particularly on inequality

00:34:51.460 --> 00:34:53.649
and social immobility.

00:34:53.649 --> 00:34:57.579
Thank you very much for all your service.

00:34:57.579 --> 00:35:04.990
My question, since David asked so many wonderful
ones, is about the-your policies, particularly

00:35:04.990 --> 00:35:16.240
the one regarding providing workers more ability
to use unemployment insurance to integrate

00:35:16.240 --> 00:35:17.970
into a new job.

00:35:17.970 --> 00:35:23.420
Now, this relates to the TPP as well, and
the trade adjustment assistance, which over

00:35:23.420 --> 00:35:29.790
the years has had many proposals, all of which
have-(laughs)-either not been properly funded

00:35:29.790 --> 00:35:32.329
or have not been put into place.

00:35:32.329 --> 00:35:39.450
Do we need a whole new program for this economy
for what I guess I would consider the gig

00:35:39.450 --> 00:35:44.910
economy, where everybody is kind of coming
in and going out and there's lots of churn,

00:35:44.910 --> 00:35:47.579
and certainly the need to prepare?

00:35:47.579 --> 00:35:51.150
Is there a program there that you would put
as a priority?

00:35:51.150 --> 00:35:52.690
FURMAN: Mmm hmm.

00:35:52.690 --> 00:35:55.510
So I think the-I think that's a great question.

00:35:55.510 --> 00:35:56.540
We're doing one at time, right?

00:35:56.540 --> 00:35:57.540
RUBENSTEIN: One at a time.

00:35:57.540 --> 00:35:58.540
FURMAN: OK, great.

00:35:58.540 --> 00:35:59.980
RUBENSTEIN: How come you didn't say any of
my questions were great?

00:35:59.980 --> 00:36:01.690
But, geez, OK.

00:36:01.690 --> 00:36:02.690
(Laughter.)

00:36:02.690 --> 00:36:06.820
That must have been a really good question.

00:36:06.820 --> 00:36:12.030
FURMAN: And what you're asking is very much
the motivation for a lot of the unemployment

00:36:12.030 --> 00:36:13.500
insurance reforms we're talking about.

00:36:13.500 --> 00:36:19.109
If you look at the fraction of people who
aren't-who are unemployed, but are getting,

00:36:19.109 --> 00:36:23.210
are eligible for unemployment insurance, it's
much lower today than it was in the past,

00:36:23.210 --> 00:36:26.260
in part because of those economic developments
you said.

00:36:26.260 --> 00:36:30.060
So one thing is to make it, for example, if
you work part time, or if you have a shorter

00:36:30.060 --> 00:36:34.280
work history, or things like that, that you're
more eligible for unemployment insurance.

00:36:34.280 --> 00:36:38.850
And the particular line you called that there
was something where we'd like to have states

00:36:38.850 --> 00:36:44.200
to have more flexibility to do innovative
things with their unemployment insurance including

00:36:44.200 --> 00:36:49.530
not just you get it when you don't have a
job, but let's say you get an apprenticeship

00:36:49.530 --> 00:36:52.890
that might lead to a job, maybe you carry
some of that money with you.

00:36:52.890 --> 00:36:57.349
That will be an incentive for the employer
to hire you, an incentive for you to go onto

00:36:57.349 --> 00:36:59.240
and get you back into the workforce.

00:36:59.240 --> 00:37:01.510
And we've seen that successfully in some states.

00:37:01.510 --> 00:37:03.359
And that has to be done, you know, carefully.

00:37:03.359 --> 00:37:05.820
There's a lot of different twists and turns
in it.

00:37:05.820 --> 00:37:10.140
But it's something we think, you know, is
a sensible thing to incorporate within the

00:37:10.140 --> 00:37:11.280
existing program.

00:37:11.280 --> 00:37:12.430
RUBENSTEIN: OK.

00:37:12.430 --> 00:37:13.430
Back here.

00:37:13.430 --> 00:37:15.910
Stand up and please identify yourself.

00:37:15.910 --> 00:37:17.829
And a mic.

00:37:17.829 --> 00:37:18.829
Q: Thank you.

00:37:18.829 --> 00:37:20.220
Adam Taylor with the World Bank Group.

00:37:20.220 --> 00:37:21.220
So thank you.

00:37:21.220 --> 00:37:22.220
Great overview.

00:37:22.220 --> 00:37:26.260
I'm curious what happens when you separate
your data by race.

00:37:26.260 --> 00:37:27.540
You mentioned gender.

00:37:27.540 --> 00:37:31.099
But there are pronounced disparities in the
unemployment rate between black, white, Hispanic,

00:37:31.099 --> 00:37:32.099
et cetera.

00:37:32.099 --> 00:37:37.080
I'm curious if they're as pronounced in the
male, prime labor participation rate.

00:37:37.080 --> 00:37:41.960
And if so, does the more general approach
of policy prescriptions that you outline,

00:37:41.960 --> 00:37:42.960
is that enough?

00:37:42.960 --> 00:37:48.930
Or does it require much more targeted prescriptions
in order to accelerate the participation rate

00:37:48.930 --> 00:37:49.980
in order to catch up?

00:37:49.980 --> 00:37:50.980
FURMAN: All right.

00:37:50.980 --> 00:37:52.710
So thanks for that question.

00:37:52.710 --> 00:37:57.080
We do have a chart which shows the prime-age
male labor force participation by race in

00:37:57.080 --> 00:37:58.080
the report.

00:37:58.080 --> 00:38:01.770
And I think there were copies of the report
floating around.

00:38:01.770 --> 00:38:06.910
The basic thing that you see, which you probably
wouldn't be surprised, is that for black men

00:38:06.910 --> 00:38:12.600
their labor force participation rate has fallen
a bit more than it has for white men, and

00:38:12.600 --> 00:38:14.730
is a decent amount lower.

00:38:14.730 --> 00:38:17.310
Hispanics, interestingly, it's been the opposite.

00:38:17.310 --> 00:38:20.000
It's risen a little bit or been more stable.

00:38:20.000 --> 00:38:24.869
And so Hispanic labor force participation
for prime-age men is now a tiny bit higher

00:38:24.869 --> 00:38:26.780
than it is for white men.

00:38:26.780 --> 00:38:30.760
That's also part of a broader fact that immigrants
are more likely to participate than non-immigrants,

00:38:30.760 --> 00:38:36.450
which is one of, you know, the many economic
benefits of immigration reform.

00:38:36.450 --> 00:38:40.190
In terms of the implications of that for policy,
certainly it says things like My Brother's

00:38:40.190 --> 00:38:44.980
Keeper are-you know, that are more targeted
are very important.

00:38:44.980 --> 00:38:48.680
But I wouldn't lose the bigger, macro issue
either.

00:38:48.680 --> 00:38:55.530
If you look at the Great Recession, the biggest
impact on unemployment was on-you know, was

00:38:55.530 --> 00:38:56.700
on African-Americans.

00:38:56.700 --> 00:39:03.091
The unemployment rate went up from the mid
9s, I think, to about 16, which is-which is

00:39:03.091 --> 00:39:04.091
terrible.

00:39:04.091 --> 00:39:09.000
If you look at the recovery, the unemployment
rate for African-Americans actually fell more

00:39:09.000 --> 00:39:11.880
quickly than the overall unemployment rate.

00:39:11.880 --> 00:39:18.450
So there is a first to be fired, but also
a, you know, are benefitted by a broader economic

00:39:18.450 --> 00:39:19.460
recovery and aggregate demand.

00:39:19.460 --> 00:39:22.730
So I wouldn't lose sight of the aggregate
policies at all.

00:39:22.730 --> 00:39:23.730
RUBENSTEIN: OK.

00:39:23.730 --> 00:39:24.730
Right here.

00:39:24.730 --> 00:39:26.900
Q: Good afternoon.

00:39:26.900 --> 00:39:30.260
Nicole Lamb-Hale, from Albright Stonebridge
Group.

00:39:30.260 --> 00:39:36.540
What's the impact of the shared economy or
the sharing economy on U.S. economic outlook?

00:39:36.540 --> 00:39:43.160
FURMAN: It's a-you have a better question
than I have an answer, which is we don't have

00:39:43.160 --> 00:39:47.940
really great data to track that sector of
the economy.

00:39:47.940 --> 00:39:54.200
Right now, it doesn't seem actually that large
relative to GDP, as it is relative to, you

00:39:54.200 --> 00:40:01.590
know, news coverage and discussion of it,
or our own felt experience with it.

00:40:01.590 --> 00:40:06.050
My expectation is that for the most part it
represents, you know, a set of innovations

00:40:06.050 --> 00:40:09.470
and new sets of innovations are what you need
for productivity growth.

00:40:09.470 --> 00:40:12.829
And that will help our economy.

00:40:12.829 --> 00:40:19.900
Overall, it's created some challenges as well
if part of what's going on is regulatory arbitrage

00:40:19.900 --> 00:40:22.270
and a way of, you know, unleveling the playing
field.

00:40:22.270 --> 00:40:26.810
But even some of that is good if you take,
you know, a city that has fewer medallions-taxi

00:40:26.810 --> 00:40:32.849
medallions today than it did in 1945 and give
people another way to travel around that circumvents,

00:40:32.849 --> 00:40:35.660
you know, a captured system like that.

00:40:35.660 --> 00:40:38.660
That breaks down a barrier to growth.

00:40:38.660 --> 00:40:39.660
But I don't have any quantification.

00:40:39.660 --> 00:40:44.319
RUBENSTEIN: By the way, on companies like
Uber and smartphones, all the other technological

00:40:44.319 --> 00:40:49.530
devices we now use and services, why has that
not produced greater productivity in our economy?

00:40:49.530 --> 00:40:51.110
We don't see great productivity growth.

00:40:51.110 --> 00:40:52.110
Why is that?

00:40:52.110 --> 00:40:55.500
FURMAN: I think some of it is it just isn't
that large a fraction of what we do.

00:40:55.500 --> 00:41:02.859
So we spend I think something like 15 times
more a year on air travel than we do on taxi

00:41:02.859 --> 00:41:04.369
and livery service.

00:41:04.369 --> 00:41:09.720
So we're all really excited about, you know,
our Uber, but it, itself, within the transportation

00:41:09.720 --> 00:41:15.810
sector, is just a small fraction of it, which
itself is a smaller fraction of everything

00:41:15.810 --> 00:41:16.810
else.

00:41:16.810 --> 00:41:17.810
And that's just true.

00:41:17.810 --> 00:41:24.460
If you just add up GDP, GDP is building houses,
educating people, treating people in health

00:41:24.460 --> 00:41:25.460
care.

00:41:25.460 --> 00:41:29.619
There are a bunch of big sectors like that
and all the exciting stuff in Silicon Valley

00:41:29.619 --> 00:41:32.390
is still a relatively small part of it overall.

00:41:32.390 --> 00:41:34.310
RUBENSTEIN: And back here?

00:41:34.310 --> 00:41:36.700
Get the mic right there.

00:41:36.700 --> 00:41:37.700
Q: (Inaudible.)

00:41:37.700 --> 00:41:39.579
RUBENSTEIN: And speak up.

00:41:39.579 --> 00:41:43.250
Q: My question has to do with the things you
just mentioned.

00:41:43.250 --> 00:41:46.420
Do you think we're in the middle of a tech
bubble?

00:41:46.420 --> 00:41:50.120
FURMAN: You should ask the markets person
here that question.

00:41:50.120 --> 00:41:51.120
(Laughter.)

00:41:51.120 --> 00:41:52.120
(Inaudible)-a markets person.

00:41:52.120 --> 00:41:53.120
RUBENSTEIN: Right here.

00:41:53.120 --> 00:41:55.520
Q: Do you think we're in a tech bubble?

00:41:55.520 --> 00:42:02.270
FURMAN: I think policymakers have done a notoriously
poor job trying to identify bubbles.

00:42:02.270 --> 00:42:09.490
And part of our goal in policy is a broader
one of strengthening the fundamentals of the

00:42:09.490 --> 00:42:10.490
economy.

00:42:10.490 --> 00:42:13.559
I think there's a lot of really exciting things
going on in tech.

00:42:13.559 --> 00:42:18.250
You know, as to the valuation of any particular
sector, I would completely defer to you, David.

00:42:18.250 --> 00:42:23.940
RUBENSTEIN: I think that there are a fair
number of so-called unicorns that are not

00:42:23.940 --> 00:42:28.510
going to be going public at the valuation
they currently exist at.

00:42:28.510 --> 00:42:36.780
And I cannot possibly-what I think happened,
honestly, is that it used to be the case in

00:42:36.780 --> 00:42:42.250
the technology world that if you were building
up a company you went-you took it public.

00:42:42.250 --> 00:42:47.940
And when you took it public, that's when the
founders could get some profits and the investors

00:42:47.940 --> 00:42:49.329
get profits.

00:42:49.329 --> 00:42:54.000
When Facebook came along, for the first time
ever, in a meaningful way, people were able

00:42:54.000 --> 00:42:59.320
to invest in the company significantly, buy
founders out and early investors out, and

00:42:59.320 --> 00:43:01.810
provide liquidity without having to go public.

00:43:01.810 --> 00:43:06.920
And those people that went in at $20 billion
and $30 billion-and Goldman had around a $50

00:43:06.920 --> 00:43:09.720
billion valuation-they still made staggering
amounts of money.

00:43:09.720 --> 00:43:13.589
So when that happened people said, I can invest
in these companies pre-IPO.

00:43:13.589 --> 00:43:18.040
I don't have to wait for an IPO to cash out,
and I'm happy to have very high valuations,

00:43:18.040 --> 00:43:19.410
because eventually they're all going to be
like Facebook.

00:43:19.410 --> 00:43:24.859
And I think that produced a phenomenon where
everybody raced into these technology companies.

00:43:24.859 --> 00:43:28.510
And while there are only a few Ubers-which
clearly are very unique companies-I don't

00:43:28.510 --> 00:43:30.140
think they're all going to be Ubers or Facebooks.

00:43:30.140 --> 00:43:33.300
So I do think that you're going to see some
of the air going out of some of these companies,

00:43:33.300 --> 00:43:36.890
as I think you've already seen, but there
will be some truly transformative companies

00:43:36.890 --> 00:43:37.890
like Uber.

00:43:37.890 --> 00:43:40.720
Now, Uber's latest valuation is $62 billion.

00:43:40.720 --> 00:43:44.100
So if you just went into the latest round
at $62 billion, to double your money you've

00:43:44.100 --> 00:43:46.050
got to have $120 billion valuation.

00:43:46.050 --> 00:43:48.230
And, you know, that's a high valuation.

00:43:48.230 --> 00:43:50.250
There aren't that many companies in the world
with that kind of valuation.

00:43:50.250 --> 00:43:54.730
But I do think that there are going to be
some people disappointed in these technology

00:43:54.730 --> 00:43:56.400
companies but some people will make some money.

00:43:56.400 --> 00:43:58.720
That's probably a longer answer than you wanted
and maybe not as satisfying.

00:43:58.720 --> 00:44:00.130
FURMAN: Which one will you bid?

00:44:00.130 --> 00:44:01.130
(Laughter.)

00:44:01.130 --> 00:44:06.579
RUBENSTEIN: Well, I generally think professional
investors just have a hard time going into

00:44:06.579 --> 00:44:08.599
these companies with these very high valuations.

00:44:08.599 --> 00:44:13.800
We probably would rather miss on the upside
than be looking like an idiot and going down.

00:44:13.800 --> 00:44:17.869
So I think you're not seeing a lot of professional
investors, pension funds, and others going

00:44:17.869 --> 00:44:20.670
into these high valuations, but there are
individual investors that make a lot of money

00:44:20.670 --> 00:44:23.390
out of Facebook and some other pre-IPO deals.

00:44:23.390 --> 00:44:26.180
And, you know, so there's some people taking
bets on them.

00:44:26.180 --> 00:44:28.820
But a lot of money that's coming in now is
foreign money.

00:44:28.820 --> 00:44:33.750
A lot of people around the world observed
that Facebook did very well, and they don't

00:44:33.750 --> 00:44:35.830
have an equivalent of Facebook in their own
country.

00:44:35.830 --> 00:44:38.099
So a lot of the money that's coming in now
is foreign money.

00:44:38.099 --> 00:44:41.230
It's sovereign wealth fund money, foreign
pension fund money, and high net-worth money

00:44:41.230 --> 00:44:45.690
coming in to, I think, build up the valuations
of some of these companies.

00:44:45.690 --> 00:44:47.609
Yes, right here.

00:44:47.609 --> 00:44:51.770
Q: Odeh Aburdene, the Capital Group.

00:44:51.770 --> 00:44:58.320
Jason, in 2008-2009 David was very negative
on European banks.

00:44:58.320 --> 00:45:02.270
He told me to short them-the Credit Suisse
and Deutsch Bank.

00:45:02.270 --> 00:45:04.480
These banks continue to have problems.

00:45:04.480 --> 00:45:06.920
If you look at their price today, they're
way down.

00:45:06.920 --> 00:45:12.349
What is the outlook for these huge European
banks in view of the economic slowdown in

00:45:12.349 --> 00:45:13.349
Europe?

00:45:13.349 --> 00:45:18.870
FURMAN: Look, I think if you're listing the
things people are worried about in the global

00:45:18.870 --> 00:45:24.210
economy, the European banks and certainly
the banks in certain countries in Europe are

00:45:24.210 --> 00:45:30.180
unfortunately on that list in a way that U.S.
banks aren't.

00:45:30.180 --> 00:45:36.470
And that's a reflection of we very early,
you know, did a stress test and did an honest

00:45:36.470 --> 00:45:39.780
accounting of what the capital needs were
in our banks.

00:45:39.780 --> 00:45:46.660
We put a lot of public effort and complementary
private effort into recapitalizing the banks,

00:45:46.660 --> 00:45:53.240
and now they have, you know, not just more
capital but also financial situations that

00:45:53.240 --> 00:45:55.760
people are reasonably trusting of.

00:45:55.760 --> 00:45:59.300
Europe has gotten a decent amount better in
that regard.

00:45:59.300 --> 00:46:06.690
They eventually, after a few attempts, got
their stress tests basically right.

00:46:06.690 --> 00:46:11.650
They have seen more capital.

00:46:11.650 --> 00:46:21.530
It's more on, you know, some of the issues
on the asset side of some of their balance

00:46:21.530 --> 00:46:22.530
sheets.

00:46:22.530 --> 00:46:29.920
But, you know, I think it does remain a concern
and something that they need to focus on.

00:46:29.920 --> 00:46:30.920
RUBENSTEIN: Great.

00:46:30.920 --> 00:46:31.920
Yes, back here?

00:46:31.920 --> 00:46:32.920
Q: Great.

00:46:32.920 --> 00:46:35.859
Tom Bollyky from the Council on Foreign Relations.

00:46:35.859 --> 00:46:37.630
Thank you for the great conversation.

00:46:37.630 --> 00:46:40.770
You brought up the Trans-Pacific Partnership
and trade.

00:46:40.770 --> 00:46:47.120
And the Council of Economic Advisers has put
out-or in the past-reports including a full-throated

00:46:47.120 --> 00:46:51.790
defense of how the U.S. has done under previous
trade agreements, including NAFTA.

00:46:51.790 --> 00:46:56.220
One of the things that was striking about
that is it's different from the way the TPP

00:46:56.220 --> 00:47:01.869
has been sold by the administration as a whole,
which starts with the premise typically that

00:47:01.869 --> 00:47:07.270
the way the U.S. has done under recent trade
agreements including NAFTA has been problematic,

00:47:07.270 --> 00:47:10.670
but that that those problems are addressed
in the TPP and that's the reason why we should

00:47:10.670 --> 00:47:11.940
move forward.

00:47:11.940 --> 00:47:17.780
Why hasn't the more full-throated defense
of how the U.S. has done in previous trade

00:47:17.780 --> 00:47:21.040
agreements, including NAFTA, been part of
the selling job?

00:47:21.040 --> 00:47:26.730
Can we move forward TPP while disavowing NAFTA
and previous U.S. trade agreements?

00:47:26.730 --> 00:47:32.450
FURMAN: So we haven't done anything that is
explicitly an analysis of any given previous

00:47:32.450 --> 00:47:35.460
trade agreement, including NAFTA.

00:47:35.460 --> 00:47:41.350
What we've talked about is the overall large
benefits of trade in general and of expanding

00:47:41.350 --> 00:47:48.280
trade: how tariffs have come down over time,
how the benefits are, in terms of producers

00:47:48.280 --> 00:47:53.750
and export jobs, paying more, but also the
very progressive benefits on the consumption

00:47:53.750 --> 00:47:56.260
side as well.

00:47:56.260 --> 00:48:01.150
So I think making that broader case on trade
is important, but I also think it's really

00:48:01.150 --> 00:48:06.990
important that TPP-no one in Congress will
have an up or down vote on trade or globalization.

00:48:06.990 --> 00:48:09.829
That's not the thing that anyone is being
asked to vote on.

00:48:09.829 --> 00:48:14.359
They're being asked to vote on a specific
agreement with a specific text and specific

00:48:14.359 --> 00:48:15.359
provisions.

00:48:15.359 --> 00:48:18.680
I think it's very important to keep the focus
on that.

00:48:18.680 --> 00:48:25.309
And so whatever your views about all these
bigger, broader issues-you know, what do you

00:48:25.309 --> 00:48:30.819
think about whether Malaysia should have stricter
labor laws than it has now?

00:48:30.819 --> 00:48:36.069
What do you think about whether Vietnam should
have rules about state-owned enterprises that

00:48:36.069 --> 00:48:37.530
they don't have now?

00:48:37.530 --> 00:48:41.880
What do you think about, you know, Brunei
needs to have, you know, an open internet,

00:48:41.880 --> 00:48:46.810
that Singapore will be party to an agreement-a
side agreement on currency?

00:48:46.810 --> 00:48:51.930
You know, that's the questions that people
are going to need to vote on and need to make

00:48:51.930 --> 00:48:53.599
their decision on.

00:48:53.599 --> 00:48:58.619
And there, you know, in every one of the respects
I just cited, this agreement, you know, moves

00:48:58.619 --> 00:49:00.380
forward from any of the previous ones.

00:49:00.380 --> 00:49:04.780
So regardless of your views on them, this
is a way to have a more level playing field.

00:49:04.780 --> 00:49:07.090
And that's the argument we've been trying
to make.

00:49:07.090 --> 00:49:10.911
RUBENSTEIN: They should have considered for
TPP an acronym that would sound better, like

00:49:10.911 --> 00:49:16.010
YES, Y-E-S, or GOOD, G-O-O-D-(laugher)-something
that, when you say it, it sounds really good.

00:49:16.010 --> 00:49:19.440
"TPP" doesn't have-doesn't, you know, go off
your tongue that easy.

00:49:19.440 --> 00:49:20.440
(Laughter.)

00:49:20.440 --> 00:49:22.190
OK, yes, right here.

00:49:22.190 --> 00:49:23.300
Q: Hi.

00:49:23.300 --> 00:49:24.610
Nelson Cunningham, McLarty Associates.

00:49:24.610 --> 00:49:26.829
Thanks for coming and doing this, Jason.

00:49:26.829 --> 00:49:32.940
On page 6, the institutional explanations
with the OECD numbers, the numbers all sort

00:49:32.940 --> 00:49:39.930
of roughly made sense to me except for the
very top one, which is "Barriers to Entrepreneurship."

00:49:39.930 --> 00:49:45.660
And the U.S. had a number of 62, which seems
to suggest in the world, or at least in the

00:49:45.660 --> 00:49:50.559
OECD world, that it's sort of in the middle
ranks of countries in terms of entrepreneurship,

00:49:50.559 --> 00:49:55.400
which is certainly not how I think of the
U.S. compared to the other OECD countries.

00:49:55.400 --> 00:49:56.859
FURMAN: Yep.

00:49:56.859 --> 00:49:59.339
Q: Can you explain that number to us?

00:49:59.339 --> 00:50:04.630
FURMAN: You know, I was puzzled by that too
and have meant to look into it more and don't

00:50:04.630 --> 00:50:09.880
actually know the answer, so-but I'll get
back to you.

00:50:09.880 --> 00:50:12.640
RUBENSTEIN: OK, back here.

00:50:12.640 --> 00:50:14.720
Q: Thank you.

00:50:14.720 --> 00:50:15.720
Rich Miller with Bloomberg.

00:50:15.720 --> 00:50:17.010
Thanks very much for the presentation.

00:50:17.010 --> 00:50:20.220
You alluded to this before, but I wondered
if you could expand on the role of immigration

00:50:20.220 --> 00:50:27.800
in-both legal and illegal-in the fall in participation
rate, especially among the lesser-educated.

00:50:27.800 --> 00:50:30.810
FURMAN: Mmm hmm.

00:50:30.810 --> 00:50:36.339
Immigration has, you know, mostly likely helped
the labor force participation rate.

00:50:36.339 --> 00:50:38.480
There's two aspects of it.

00:50:38.480 --> 00:50:43.089
One is, people tend to come to this country
more in their working years.

00:50:43.089 --> 00:50:45.970
You don't get a lot of 80-year old immigrants.

00:50:45.970 --> 00:50:53.090
And you don't get a whole lot of, you know,
children coming here on their own either.

00:50:53.090 --> 00:50:58.250
So the group of people you get is more demographically
favorable.

00:50:58.250 --> 00:51:03.700
And then they're people who are-you know,
tend to be working really hard to be successful

00:51:03.700 --> 00:51:08.900
and incorporate themselves into the U.S. economy,
and as a result tend to participate at a higher

00:51:08.900 --> 00:51:11.619
rate than people that were born here.

00:51:11.619 --> 00:51:14.950
So I think it has been something that's helped
it.

00:51:14.950 --> 00:51:20.829
And if we had comprehensive, common-sense
immigration reform, it would continue to help

00:51:20.829 --> 00:51:24.920
it going forward, both in terms of the age
of the people you're bringing, or propensity

00:51:24.920 --> 00:51:30.420
to immigrate, but also the positive spillovers
that immigration has for other workers in

00:51:30.420 --> 00:51:34.359
the economy, for their wages, and for growth
overall.

00:51:34.359 --> 00:51:37.400
Q: But what would you think about the argument
that some would make, is that when you have

00:51:37.400 --> 00:51:41.750
more immigration the immigrants are willing
to work at lower prices or lower salaries,

00:51:41.750 --> 00:51:46.040
so therefore it's a disincentive for, let's
say, native-born Americans who don't want

00:51:46.040 --> 00:51:48.510
to work at these lower prices, therefore they
just say I'll stay at home.

00:51:48.510 --> 00:51:50.500
What do you think of that argument?

00:51:50.500 --> 00:51:54.559
FURMAN: There's been-a lot of people have
taken a look at large influxes of immigrants,

00:51:54.559 --> 00:52:01.960
whether it was from Cuba during the Mariel
boatlift, Algerians to France, Russian Jews

00:52:01.960 --> 00:52:07.369
to Israel-you tend to see very little impact
of wages of people in the countries, that

00:52:07.369 --> 00:52:10.760
those large influxes of immigration come to.

00:52:10.760 --> 00:52:14.690
And that's because a lot of what immigrants
are doing actually complement the things that

00:52:14.690 --> 00:52:15.800
native workers are doing.

00:52:15.800 --> 00:52:19.420
I think it's also important that when you
look at something like common sense immigration

00:52:19.420 --> 00:52:22.010
reform, that's not even what we're talking
about.

00:52:22.010 --> 00:52:26.300
Most of what we're talking about is taking
the 11 million people who are here, who are

00:52:26.300 --> 00:52:32.559
not documented, and giving them a path to
participate in the workforce and to become

00:52:32.559 --> 00:52:33.890
citizens.

00:52:33.890 --> 00:52:37.950
And that means they'll have more labor rules
applied to them, if anything will be less

00:52:37.950 --> 00:52:39.480
able to bargain wages down.

00:52:39.480 --> 00:52:42.970
RUBENSTEIN: And when I introduced you, I didn't
mention that your freshman year roommate was

00:52:42.970 --> 00:52:43.970
Matt Damon.

00:52:43.970 --> 00:52:44.970
Is that true?

00:52:44.970 --> 00:52:46.800
FURMAN: I was grateful for that omission,
but it is true.

00:52:46.800 --> 00:52:48.450
RUBENSTEIN: And what was he like?

00:52:48.450 --> 00:52:49.450
(Laughter.)

00:52:49.450 --> 00:52:54.170
Did you know he was going to be famous-
FURMAN: He was-I think in literally the first

00:52:54.170 --> 00:52:58.970
conversation we had in front of Matthews in
Harvard Yard he told me I was really lucky

00:52:58.970 --> 00:53:03.030
to have him as a roommate, because he was
almost certain to become a movie star.

00:53:03.030 --> 00:53:06.069
And that wouldn't mean-he didn't mean in the
sense that years later you could have this

00:53:06.069 --> 00:53:09.770
in my thing, the more practical that he'd
be leaving our room in the middle of the year

00:53:09.770 --> 00:53:14.190
and it would be too late to replace him, so
I would have the whole thing to myself.

00:53:14.190 --> 00:53:16.510
But it took him a little bit longer than he
had projected.

00:53:16.510 --> 00:53:17.510
RUBENSTEIN: OK.

00:53:17.510 --> 00:53:18.510
All right.

00:53:18.510 --> 00:53:20.890
Is he inviting you to any movie openings,
or he doesn't invite you to any movie openings?

00:53:20.890 --> 00:53:23.339
FURMAN: We see each other from time to time.

00:53:23.339 --> 00:53:24.420
RUBENSTEIN: OK.

00:53:24.420 --> 00:53:26.020
Right here, Lloyd.

00:53:26.020 --> 00:53:28.700
Q: Lloyd Hand, King &amp; Spalding.

00:53:28.700 --> 00:53:34.460
I happened to be in the audience, because
our granddaughter graduated from MIT, when

00:53:34.460 --> 00:53:36.809
Matt Damon made the commencement address.

00:53:36.809 --> 00:53:38.300
He was terrific.

00:53:38.300 --> 00:53:43.250
He had a particular poignant comment-
FURMAN: Did he get introduced as my former

00:53:43.250 --> 00:53:44.250
roommate?

00:53:44.250 --> 00:53:45.250
(Laughter.)

00:53:45.250 --> 00:53:49.270
They thought maybe he had enough-
Q: He may have missed that part.

00:53:49.270 --> 00:53:57.349
But for this particular audience, for which
the word nerd is an understood, he said: Before

00:53:57.349 --> 00:54:02.390
people care about what you know, they want
to know about whether you care.

00:54:02.390 --> 00:54:04.640
I thought that was a pretty good point.

00:54:04.640 --> 00:54:05.640
He made a terrific speech.

00:54:05.640 --> 00:54:06.890
It was a good mixture.

00:54:06.890 --> 00:54:13.170
My question, you sort of demurred when David
asked you earlier about what policy prescriptions

00:54:13.170 --> 00:54:15.540
you may have for future leaders.

00:54:15.540 --> 00:54:17.420
I have a related question.

00:54:17.420 --> 00:54:21.240
FURMAN: I just didn't want to take, like,
a couple hours and it deserved a more succinct

00:54:21.240 --> 00:54:24.540
answer than I was capable-
Q: Of course, that would depend upon which

00:54:24.540 --> 00:54:26.740
person you were advising.

00:54:26.740 --> 00:54:34.160
But my question is this, as you-and you may
want to take your chairman's hat off to be

00:54:34.160 --> 00:54:36.460
able to answer this.

00:54:36.460 --> 00:54:44.950
But are there any particular issues, factors,
concerns that you've learned expressed from

00:54:44.950 --> 00:54:52.891
the electorate-not DNR, but just that surprise
you and that provide maybe some lessons learned?

00:54:52.891 --> 00:54:59.950
FURMAN: You know, I just-I mean, I don't know
that I'm surprised by it, but there's a decent

00:54:59.950 --> 00:55:05.210
amount of economic discontent, as we all know,
and as we see affecting things.

00:55:05.210 --> 00:55:09.369
The combination of the tax changes we've done
in this administration, both cutting taxes

00:55:09.369 --> 00:55:15.410
at the bottom, raising them at the top, and
the Affordable Care Act are the biggest, you

00:55:15.410 --> 00:55:20.630
know, progressive change-or among the biggest
progressive policy changes we've had in decades.

00:55:20.630 --> 00:55:23.579
So they've really done a lot.

00:55:23.579 --> 00:55:28.240
The economy's in, you know, much better shape
than it was in eight years ago.

00:55:28.240 --> 00:55:30.099
But you still have a lot of discontent.

00:55:30.099 --> 00:55:34.800
And I think some of that is wages aren't growing
as quickly as you'd like them to grow.

00:55:34.800 --> 00:55:39.460
But some of it is this issue we're discussing
here today, when 60 years ago 2 percent of

00:55:39.460 --> 00:55:42.310
men weren't in the workforce and now it's
12 percent of men.

00:55:42.310 --> 00:55:43.390
That's a lot of people.

00:55:43.390 --> 00:55:50.430
And so it can be for your typical person things
are just fine, but you have a very large group

00:55:50.430 --> 00:55:52.780
for whom it is very far from fine.

00:55:52.780 --> 00:55:58.490
You know, I think that's screaming out for
attention and screaming out for us to do something

00:55:58.490 --> 00:55:59.530
about it.

00:55:59.530 --> 00:56:01.609
RUBENSTEIN: Yes, right here.

00:56:01.609 --> 00:56:05.250
Q: The question-
RUBENSTEIN: We have-take a-

00:56:05.250 --> 00:56:08.390
Q: Just one theory on the entrepreneurship.

00:56:08.390 --> 00:56:13.550
We had the lowest level of people starting
businesses I think in the last 60 years.

00:56:13.550 --> 00:56:17.099
And some of the explanation-my name's Kathleen
Kennedy Townsend.

00:56:17.099 --> 00:56:20.920
And some of the explanation is that a lot
of students are-have so much student debt

00:56:20.920 --> 00:56:26.500
that they can't access money that they would
have accessed to start businesses.

00:56:26.500 --> 00:56:31.290
That's one of the challenges on entrepreneurship
that we've looked at.

00:56:31.290 --> 00:56:35.569
What I was going to ask you about is that
union membership and how you can help with

00:56:35.569 --> 00:56:36.569
that.

00:56:36.569 --> 00:56:43.380
Because I think this is related to both income,
on one hand, and also social solidarity.

00:56:43.380 --> 00:56:47.559
As you know, the group that you're working
with is also committing suicide at larger

00:56:47.559 --> 00:56:52.960
rates than they ever had before, comparable
to the AIDS epidemic in the 1980s.

00:56:52.960 --> 00:56:59.650
And part of-as union-being part of a union
raises your happiness levels, which I'm sure

00:56:59.650 --> 00:57:01.420
you've seen the numbers on that.

00:57:01.420 --> 00:57:07.249
So the extent that you can up unions' membership,
you get two-you got a twofer.

00:57:07.249 --> 00:57:12.220
People are happier, and, two, they can get
better benefits.

00:57:12.220 --> 00:57:14.339
So I'd love you to talk about that.

00:57:14.339 --> 00:57:15.339
FURMAN: Yeah.

00:57:15.339 --> 00:57:16.900
So I agree with that on substance.

00:57:16.900 --> 00:57:22.700
And the CEA put out an issue brief on unions
and voice-worker voice in the economy more

00:57:22.700 --> 00:57:29.020
broadly about six months ago that, you know,
brought together a lot of the evidence in

00:57:29.020 --> 00:57:30.020
that area.

00:57:30.020 --> 00:57:33.480
But a lot of what you do there isn't rocket
science.

00:57:33.480 --> 00:57:37.580
It's changing a bunch of detailed rules about
how people signed up.

00:57:37.580 --> 00:57:41.690
Certainly in the first two years of this administration,
the Employee Free Choice Act was before Congress.

00:57:41.690 --> 00:57:46.460
It's something that, you know, couldn't get
60 votes in the Senate so it didn't make it

00:57:46.460 --> 00:57:47.770
to the president's desk.

00:57:47.770 --> 00:57:52.121
Since then we've tried to take steps like
the NLRB, and making sure that that's there

00:57:52.121 --> 00:57:55.960
and that that's protected, and that that's
protecting people's ability to organize.

00:57:55.960 --> 00:57:56.960
RUBENSTEIN: OK.

00:57:56.960 --> 00:57:58.650
We have time for one more question.

00:57:58.650 --> 00:58:01.920
Carla, our co-chairman on the Council, will
have the last question.

00:58:01.920 --> 00:58:04.050
Q: Thank you, David.

00:58:04.050 --> 00:58:07.250
Carla Hills, Hills &amp; Company, international
consultants.

00:58:07.250 --> 00:58:14.040
I thought your presentation on the labor participation
was outstanding.

00:58:14.040 --> 00:58:21.109
And we're having greater manufacturing output
with fewer workers.

00:58:21.109 --> 00:58:29.520
But we also have a recording of job shortages-serious
job shortages, which don't need a doctorate

00:58:29.520 --> 00:58:30.800
or a college degree.

00:58:30.800 --> 00:58:32.420
They need training.

00:58:32.420 --> 00:58:39.049
Is there any discussion going on about how
to get folks that have been dislodged from

00:58:39.049 --> 00:58:46.130
manufacturing and are not screwing wheels
on autos, but who could come and fix my computer

00:58:46.130 --> 00:58:51.680
and connect things, but they don't need a
doctorate to do it?

00:58:51.680 --> 00:58:57.240
And is there any talk about giving a stipend,
the way that Latin American countries have

00:58:57.240 --> 00:59:03.900
enlarged their middle class, by giving a stipend
to you, a family, to send your family to school.

00:59:03.900 --> 00:59:08.980
But you don't get that stipend unless you
turn in the attendance record to show that

00:59:08.980 --> 00:59:10.720
they did go to school.

00:59:10.720 --> 00:59:11.940
And it's working.

00:59:11.940 --> 00:59:13.700
Could we do something like that?

00:59:13.700 --> 00:59:15.079
FURMAN: Mmm hmm.

00:59:15.079 --> 00:59:22.210
So I think that's exactly the types of questions
we need to asking, because as I was trying

00:59:22.210 --> 00:59:28.760
to show, just having a free market can be
a great thing in many respects, but it's not

00:59:28.760 --> 00:59:39.320
going to, by itself, help us in the way that
we fully want institutions to help make the

00:59:39.320 --> 00:59:41.090
labor market function.

00:59:41.090 --> 00:59:46.981
When it comes to training, most of the dollars
there are spent by-privately, not by the government.

00:59:46.981 --> 00:59:51.270
So part of what you want to do is work with
employers, get employers together with community

00:59:51.270 --> 00:59:56.190
colleges, having community colleges train
people for places where there is demand rather

00:59:56.190 --> 01:00:00.140
than, you know, sending their workers out
hoping-you know, the students out hoping that

01:00:00.140 --> 01:00:02.940
they have some skill that somebody else would
want.

01:00:02.940 --> 01:00:05.540
So that type of demand-driven training.

01:00:05.540 --> 01:00:09.930
The vice president headed up an effort for
the president and has done a number of these

01:00:09.930 --> 01:00:10.930
things.

01:00:10.930 --> 01:00:15.640
And we've tried to, you know, integrate them
in where we're using existing federal dollars

01:00:15.640 --> 01:00:20.550
today, as well as convene and work with others
to better coordinate.

01:00:20.550 --> 01:00:26.339
In terms of some of the bigger ideas you cited,
I think that's intriguing and, you know, we're

01:00:26.339 --> 01:00:27.339
thinking about.

01:00:27.339 --> 01:00:28.339
RUBENSTEIN: OK.

01:00:28.339 --> 01:00:33.650
So to keep the Council's happiness index high,
I'm going to end on time, 1:30.

01:00:33.650 --> 01:00:37.820
I want to thank you, Jason, for the great
job you did today, and for the great job you're

01:00:37.820 --> 01:00:40.970
doing for our country in the role as chairman
of the Council of Economic Advisors.

01:00:40.970 --> 01:00:41.970
Thank you very much.

01:00:41.970 --> 01:00:42.970
FURMAN: Thank you.

01:00:42.970 --> 01:00:43.970
(Applause.)

01:00:43.970 --> 01:00:43.971
(END)

